# .trivyignore - reviewed, time-boxed exceptions ONLY. Not a rubber stamp.
#
# Policy (see docs and NOTES.md for the full argument):
#   - Fail the pipeline on HIGH/CRITICAL. Warn (report, don't block) below that.
#   - --ignore-unfixed is applied separately in ci.yml: a CVE with no vendor
#     patch anywhere does not belong in this file - blocking forever on
#     something nobody can fix isn't a control, it's a permanently red
#     pipeline everyone learns to ignore. --ignore-unfixed handles that
#     case automatically, with no per-CVE entry needed.
#   - THIS FILE is for the different, more common case: a fix DOES exist
#     upstream, but hasn't been picked up by the specific pinned image tag
#     yet. Every entry here needs a real reason and a review-by date, or it
#     doesn't belong here.
#
# Real scan, real date, real findings - not hypothetical. Scanning
# nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.29.2-alpine (the chart's pinned smoke-test
# image) on 2026-07-25 turned up 22 unique HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs, all with a
# fix already released upstream (Alpine package builds newer than what
# this specific image tag was built against) - none of them "unfixed."
# Checked several other current, real tags first (1.25.4, 1.25.4-alpine,
# 1.27.4-alpine, 1.29.0-alpine) - every single one had this same shape of
# finding, just different counts. That's not a sign this tag was chosen
# badly; it's what scanning any statically pinned tag looks like some
# nonzero time after it was built - new CVEs get disclosed continuously,
# vendors don't retroactively patch already-published tags. The real fix
# is periodically bumping the pin, not chasing a tag that stays clean
# forever - no such tag exists. Review by 2026-08-25: re-scan, bump the
# pin if a newer build resolves these, and rebuild this list from scratch
# rather than aging entries out silently.

